
         Apache Kafka with Spring Boot

--> Introduction to Apache Kafka
        Apache Kafka is a distributed event streaming platform used to build real-time data pipelines and event-driven applications.
        Kafka is designed to:
        Handle millions of messages per second
        Store data durably
        Allow real-time consumption
        Support horizontal scaling
        👉 Common use cases:
        Microservices communication
        Event-driven systems
        Log aggregation
        Real-time analytics
        Order & notification systems

--> Kafka Core Concepts
        Producer
            Sends (publishes) messages to Kafka topics
            Does not know which consumer receives the message

        Consumer
            Reads messages from Kafka topics
            Pull-based model (consumer pulls data)

        Broker
            Kafka server that stores and manages messages
            Kafka cluster = multiple brokers

        Topic
            Logical channel where messages are sent
            Example: order-topic, payment-topic

        Partition
            Topic is split into partitions
            Enables parallel processing
            Messages inside a partition are ordered

        Offset
            Unique number for each message in a partition
            Used to track consumption state

        Consumer Group
            Multiple consumers working together
            Each partition is consumed by only one consumer in a group

        ZooKeeper / KRaft
            Old Kafka → ZooKeeper
            New Kafka → KRaft (Kafka Raft)
            Manages metadata and leader election

--> Kafka Architecture Overview
        Flow:
        Producer sends message to a topic
        Broker stores message in partition
        Consumer pulls message using offset
        Message stays in Kafka based on retention policy
        ✔ Kafka does not delete messages immediately after consumption

--> Advantages of Kafka
        High throughput & low latency
        Horizontally scalable
        Fault-tolerant (replication)
        Supports event-driven microservices
        Message replay using offsets

--> Spring Boot + Kafka
        Spring Boot provides spring-kafka library which:
            Auto-configures Kafka Producer & Consumer
            Simplifies configuration
            Uses annotations for listeners
        Key components:
            KafkaTemplate → Producer
            @KafkaListener → Consumer
            KafkaAdmin → Topic creation

--> Configuration Tasks
        bootstrap-servers → Kafka broker address
        group-id → Consumer group
        auto-offset-reset
        earliest → read from beginning
        latest → read new messages only
        Serializer / Deserializer → String / JSON

